   118	     5	
   119	     6	# Verify Acceptance Release
   120	     7	
   121	     8	## Goal
   122	     9	
   123	    10	Decide what the evidence actually proves without sharing the implementation role's authority or silently repairing the definition of done. Operate read-only by default.
   124	    11	
   125	    12	## Independence Boundary
   126	    13	
   127	    14	- Use a different session, context, or authorized human reviewer from the implementation role whenever practical.
   128	    15	- Review the original request, every decision-scoped acceptance fragment in the requested release scope, its decision semantic key, accepted version, and invalidation keys, task-local `acceptance/index.md`, project-level `acceptance/index.md`, declared human `binding.md` and `checklist.md`, approval evidence, protected-test hashes, implementation diff identity, and raw test/runtime evidence.
   129	    16	- Do not edit implementation or tests during review. If the user explicitly requests fixes, return findings to `$implement-acceptance-locked-change`, then rereview only the changed findings and affected evidence.
   130	    17	- Reuse a prior review when contract, source diff, protected-test hashes, environment identity, and evidence identity are unchanged.
   131	    18	
   132	    19	## Decision-Scoped Verification And Local Reuse
   133	    20	
   134	    21	When an SSOT governs the change, validate the `.ssot` machine source and select review work from explicit decision, acceptance, implementation, evidence, and resource edges. Do not wait for an entire SSOT stage before reviewing a completed decision-scoped acceptance fragment. Fragment-level review may establish reusable evidence and findings, but it cannot declare the whole release `READY`.
   135	    22	
   136	    23	Bind every review lane to the fragment hash, decision semantic key, accepted version, and invalidation keys it evaluates. When an upstream decision or contract changes, reverify only evidence lanes whose invalidation keys match and any downstream synthesis that consumed them. Do not rerun every evidence lane or discard unrelated signed human results. Unchanged decision refs and evidence identities remain reusable.
   137	    24	
   138	    25	After all blocking fragments and shared convergence outputs in the declared release scope are accepted, one release-review owner makes one final serial release decision. Global completeness belongs only at that final synthesis boundary; it must not block earlier fragment review.
   139	    26	
   140	    27	## Maximum-Safe Parallel Verification
   141	    28	
   142	    29	Pin the selected fragment versions/hashes, decision refs, protected-test baselines, source diff, runtime identity, evidence identities, required roles, and one release-review owner serially. Then inventory all independent review surfaces and calculate:
   143	    30	
   144	    31	```text
   145	    32	LOGICAL_LANE_TARGET = READY_INDEPENDENT_DELIVERABLES
   146	    33	ACTUAL_CONCURRENCY = min(LOGICAL_LANE_TARGET, AVAILABLE_WRITABLE_L2_SLOTS)
   147	    34	WAVE_COUNT = ceil(LOGICAL_LANE_TARGET / AVAILABLE_WRITABLE_L2_SLOTS)
   148	    35	```
   149	    36	
   150	    37	If `AVAILABLE_WRITABLE_L2_SLOTS` is zero, mark dispatch `BLOCKED`; do not divide by zero, silently downgrade the sandbox, or serialize review through an unregistered executor.
   151	    38	
   152	    39	Use separate logical lanes for every independently reviewable protected-test partition, AC evidence partition, static/unit/integration/E2E/non-functional evidence set, implementation module or diff region, human-acceptance binding/run, external sandbox, exploratory result, production signal, and rollback control. These are categories, not a fixed lane count: split them further whenever inputs and conclusions are isolated. Reuse hash-identical accepted review results rather than launching duplicate lanes.
   153	    40	
   154	    41	Worker capacity changes waves only. Do not merge review lanes because they share a feature, layer, role, repository, deadline, reviewer, or output decision. Group only a proven `write-write`, `mutable-read-write`, `single-migration`, `single-generated-artifact`, or `inseparable-transaction` conflict, and record the exact resource-level reason. Read-only access to the same frozen source or evidence is parallel-safe.
   155	    42	
   156	    43	Review lanes have read-only authority even though their process receives a writable sandbox. They must not edit implementation, tests, the contract, human results, existing evidence, or SSOT status. Give each lane a unique return/evidence path, such as `acceptance/release/runs/<run-id>/evidence/review-lanes/<lane-id>.md`; only the release-review owner writes the final `result.md`, rebuilds indexes, reconciles AC/H dispositions, and makes one serial decision after all required lanes return. A lane may report a finding but may not repair it or independently declare the release `READY`.
   157	    44	
   158	    45	Use `$codex-explicit-worker-orchestration` for every dispatched review lane.
   159	    46	Resolve and inspect the project-approved L2 wrapper first, then launch one bounded
   160	    47	task per wrapper invocation:
   161	    48	
   162	    49	If the user or project adapter prioritizes an absolute `run-lw-luna.sh`, use it
   163	    50	for eligible writable lanes and do not remap it to `run-l2.sh` or another model.
   164	    51	
   165	    52	```bash
   166	    53	/absolute/path/run-l2.sh "<bounded acceptance-review lane packet>"
   167	    54	```
   168	    55	
   169	    56	The inspected wrapper must end in `exec codex exec`, and its durable registration
   170	    57	must preserve:
   171	    58	
   172	    59	```text
   173	    60	codex exec -C /absolute/project/root --skip-git-repo-check --sandbox danger-full-access
   174	    61	```
   175	    62	
   176	    63	Record `writable sandbox` on every lane. Do not use `.`, a relative root, `$PWD`,
   177	    64	an unresolved variable, or an unrecorded wrapper invocation in place of this
   178	    65	contract. Never use `spawn_agent`, a chat-internal subagent, or a same-process
   179	    66	collaboration API as a substitute or fallback. Full filesystem capability does
   180	    67	not authorize writes outside the unique lane-return path. Each packet must include
   181	    68	task/node ID, direct parent, pinned identity tuple, exact read scope, the sole
   182	    69	allowed return path, forbidden writes, selected requirements/evidence, commands,
   183	    70	stop condition, and finding schema. The outer coordinator records command, PID,
   184	    71	Codex session, log, return path, timestamps, and exit code for each process.
   185	    72	
   186	    73	When an SSOT governs release verification, express each ready review surface as a numbered sibling after only its own evidence-freeze predecessor. Add scoped synthesis nodes for evidence that must converge and one final serial release successor for the declared release scope. Do not hide all independent review work in one node, make it wait for unrelated decisions, or let parallel reviewers mutate `ACCEPTED` state.
   187	    74	
   188	    75	## Review Workflow
   189	    76	
   190	    77	1. Validate every selected acceptance fragment and confirm it was approved by the named authority before its implementation completed. Check current SSOT decision refs and invalidation keys, then dispatch the maximum conflict-free set of ready evidence-review lanes across fragments.
   191	    78	2. Verify every protected acceptance test still exists and matches its approved hash. Treat deleted tests, weakened assertions, broad skips, unapproved snapshot updates, and fixture-specific production branches as blocking findings.
   192	    79	3. Reconcile every `AC-*` item in each fragment with fresh or still-valid evidence. Confirm the test layer is strong enough for the claim; screenshots do not prove database side effects, and mocks do not prove a real external integration. For a UI Change declaration with `user_visible: true`, absence of a valid reviewed `evidence/screenshot-manifest.json` in a passing machine E2E run is a blocking finding.
   193	    80	4. Review implementation quality: module boundaries, reuse, transaction behavior, errors, dependencies, unrelated changes, hidden fallbacks, swallowed failures, hard-coding, and observability.
   194	    81	5. Review the five machine layers proportionately: static, unit, integration/contract, critical E2E, and applicable non-functional checks.
   195	    82	6. Verify real sandbox evidence for release-critical external services in addition to mock contracts. Record account/role, environment, timestamp, resource identity, and cleanup state.
   196	    83	7. Run the acceptance artifact `check`, then read `acceptance/human-acceptance-log.json` for the exact release task IDs. Treat `PENDING`, `REJECTED`, and `INVALIDATED` blocking entries as `NOT READY`; ignore non-blocking sampling entries for the release decision. Resolve each dated project-level human workspace from the contract's unprefixed logical path. Verify its physical name has `未-` exactly when no current valid human `PASS` exists, then verify `binding.md` matches the exact task ID, SSOT path/node, contract version/hash, checklist hash, role, and blocking status. Read its stable checklist and the selected immutable `runs/<run-id>/result.md`; verify the run's contract, binding, and checklist snapshots and hashes before evaluating each signed `H-*` decision. An AI reviewer may prepare or observe the checklist but must not impersonate the product owner for blocking decisions. Queueing happens immediately after machine-green proof, but human execution belongs to a separate terminal phase and must not interrupt intermediate machine development.
   197	    84	8. Check exploratory findings, rollback readiness, and production monitoring for high-risk changes. Define the metric, threshold, window, owner, and response action; “monitor logs” is not a release control.
   198	    85	9. Verify the push-then-cleanup closeout for the declared release scope: the accepted changes are on the authoritative GitHub remote with tip readback evidence (mainline-attribution rules apply — a local-only or unverified push is not delivery), and the intermediate parallel-execution artifacts — git worktrees, candidate workspaces, codex worker runtime environments — are removed with cleanup-ledger evidence or carry explicit retention entries with owner, reason, and expiry. Unpushed accepted commits or an open cleanup ledger in release scope block `READY`; a worktree deleted while it still held unpushed or unmerged commits is a blocking finding.
   199	    86	10. Create a new task-local `acceptance/release/runs/<run-id>/` review directory for each reviewed fragment or scoped synthesis. Bind the result to decision refs/invalidation keys, contract version/hash, source identity, runtime identity, and every accepted upstream run ID plus result hash. Never overwrite an earlier review or release decision.
   200	    87	11. Rebuild and validate all affected task indexes and the project index. In an existing SSOT, reference selected machine, human, and release run IDs and hashes only from the affected nodes. Only each designated acceptance owner may move its node to `ACCEPTED`; only the release-review owner may issue the final release decision.
   201	    88	12. Write findings first using [`references/release-review-template.md`](references/release-review-template.md). Do not use test counts or code coverage as a completion claim.
   202	    89	
   203	    90	## Decision States
   204	    91	
   205	    92	- `READY`: all blocking ACs have adequate evidence, every user-visible frontend change has a valid reviewed screenshot manifest, protected tests are intact, the generated human log has no unresolved blocking entry in release scope, required human items are explicitly accepted, release controls are ready, and the push-then-cleanup closeout is verified: accepted changes confirmed on the authoritative remote by tip readback, and the release scope's worktree/workspace/worker-runtime cleanup ledger closed or carrying explicit retention entries.
   206	    93	- `CONDITIONAL`: machine evidence passes, but named non-blocking follow-up or time-bounded production evidence remains. State the exact condition and owner.
   207	    94	- `NOT READY`: any blocking AC, protected-test integrity check, required human acceptance, rollback control, or environment evidence is missing or failed.
   208	    95	- `BLOCKED`: the review cannot obtain required credentials, environment, device, approval, or authoritative input. Do not convert lack of evidence into a pass.
   209	    96	
   210	    97	## Output
   211	    98	
   212	    99	Report severity-ordered findings with file/evidence references, the AC/H traceability disposition, protected-test integrity, machine-layer results, human acceptance status, external sandbox evidence, monitoring and rollback readiness, final decision, and residual risk.
